v483f5 [RESUPPLY/DIAG - AIR-START LAND REFUSAL LOCALISED TO THE MIDDLE POS AXIS]
      The v482f5 dump caught it (server.log 22:31:02, an air-start FFA landing).
      Refused msg-119, movement=213976, last 8 samples (all native op=0x07):
        X: 45240 45133 45046 44979 44932 44903 44888 44880  (clean, rolling to a
           stop - steps 107>87>67>47>29>15>8)
        Z: 2819 2820 2820 2821 2821 2821 2821 2821           (clean, constant alt)
        Y: 44541 18941 51197 10749 28157 38909 44541 47101   (GARBAGE, 10k<->51k)
      The whole 213976 is the Y axis. Tell: every Y ends in 0xFD (0xADFD 0x49FD
      0xC7FD 0x29FD 0x6DFD 0x97FD 0xADFD 0xB7FD) - low byte PINNED, only the parsed
      high byte swings. So the middle u16 that struct.unpack_from('<HHH', pl, 9)
      reads at offset 11:13 is a stable byte (0xFD) glued to a wild byte from an
      adjacent field; X @9:11 and Z @13:15 are proper coordinates. i.e. the 3x-u16
      position parse is mis-aligned on the middle axis for this native-0x07 form.
      Explains the intermittency: the client sends its one 119 during roll-out; the
      clean exit 14s later logged movement=0 (adjacent field happened to align).
      NOT the 0x08 conversion (all samples op=0x07).
      * ADDED (read-only): the msg-119 airborne-refusal now also dumps raw
        last_plane_telem hex, to pin the TRUE position offsets before any fix.
        Likely fix once confirmed: correct the middle-axis offset (so a parked
        air-start plane reads movement 0 and the existing guard passes), OR compute
        the airborne check from the reliable axes only. Do NOT change plane_movement
        blindly - it also drives crash detection. Awaiting one raw frame.

