      delete 425' but TWO 'Receive create object 425' (the server had sent
      the object-only re-create to him twice, .131 + .360, each supposedly
      paired with a pre-delete - one pre-delete went missing across the
      stall). Create #2 hit the occupied slot -> client !Objects[N] guard
      (Network.cpp:391) -> hard exception. The allocator was CLEAN: v456's
      history grace held, 425 was never re-issued server-side - the dup was
      a re-delivery, not a re-allocation.
      * FIX: the object-only re-create is now ONE msg-13 batch carrying
        [delete-record][create-record] - a single reliable seq the client
        dispatches in order (build_msg13 is variadic; delete of a missing
        object is a proven no-op). Slot cleared-then-filled atomically;
        duplicate delivery of the whole pair self-clears. Two messages can
        always be split by loss/reorder/stalls; one cannot.
      * OPEN threads filed, not fixed: why the .360 duplicate re-create
        fired 229ms after the first (now harmless but unexplained), and
        Taurus's recurring long reliable stalls (also 23:01, 54 retx ->
        session closed) - his link genuinely drops; STATLOSS/RELKEEP now
        document it per event.

v473f5 [ARENA RENAME - PERSISTENT ACROSS RESTARTS + IN-ARENA] Web-admin
      arena renames appeared to stick only in live memory. The REAL bug was
      the reverse: /admin/edit_arena writes rooms.room_name correctly, but
      the boot-time migration RE-DERIVED room_name from the binary GAME_DEF
      blob unconditionally - every restart clobbered the rename back to the
      name stored inside game_def_raw. Three-part fix, blob never mutated:
      * Migration re-derives ONLY when the column is empty/'Unnamed' (its
        original backfill job) - the COLUMN is now display-authoritative.
      * Lobby list already reads the column (build_arenalist r[1]) - renames
        show on the next list refresh, no restart needed.
      * In-arena name: build_lz_gamedef gained room_name= (stamped at the
        212 serve site from room[1]) - LENGTH-SAFE in-place overwrite of the
        null-terminated name field at decompressed offset 20+d[13], null-
        padded within the old name's envelope so nothing shifts; a LONGER
        name is refused with a once-per-run log (lobby still shows it).

v472f5 [TELEM/0x08-CONVERT - 'UNSUPPORTED MESSAGE 8' ELIMINATED] The final
      act of the msg-8 saga. GROUND TRUTH from both binaries: FA.exe registrar
      FUN_007e5e80 hard-wires receive-table (0xcbc190) entries 8-11 to the
      'Unsupported message %i' stub while building SEND channels for all of
      7-11 -> types 8+ are client->server-only telemetry variants the 2009
      server never relayed raw. Every relayed 0x08 was a dropped tick on every
      watcher's screen (emitter stutters; 1656 (Taurus-build) + 255 (Bigalon)
      client errors in the 19:54 session alone; emitters that night: Bigalon,
      Meno, FG_BoogDog, SinneR - a client STATE, not one build). The v469
      TELEM8-TRANS capture (run_204429: same object, consecutive frames,
      ticks 13 apart = 260ms) localized the structure byte-exactly:
        0x07 payload = [pos-sample 9B][motion 21B][state 39B]  (landmark @+30)
        0x08 payload = [pos 9B][pos-sample2 9B][motion][state] (landmark @+39)
      = DOUBLE-SAMPLED position, sample2 the LATER point.
      * FIX: TELEM8_CONVERT rewrites 0x08->0x07 at the relay - opcode patched,
        one sample spliced out (TELEM8_KEEP_FIRST=False keeps the LATER,
        fresher one; flag = the A/B), size nibbles recomputed generically
        (size = bc*16 + (T>>4); -9), inconsistent-nibble frames relayed as-is
        + logged once. Output lands in TELEM_NORMAL_SIZES; all downstream
        (tick harvest, position evidence, per-recipient re-stamp) sees a
        normal 07.
      * VERIFIED run_205857 + client logs: conversions fired for Bigalon
        (21:00:10) and SinneR (21:09:43) INSIDE the sampled sessions, and
        both uploaded client logs (Dec-Developer AND Oct-Normal builds) show
        ZERO Unsupported messages of any kind. User: 'much smoother'.

v471f5 [STATLOSS - REPLY OFFSETS CORRECTED] The v470 RAW dumps decoded the
