v457f5 [TSYNC - ATTACHED TIME PINGS - ROOT CAUSE OF THE LATENCY COMPLAINT]
      Player latency complaint -> vcncnet log forensics: wire CLEAN (RTT
      109-128ms stable, ZERO reliable loss, telemetry p99 255ms) but the
      client's clock model FROZE ~60s into every session: 410 timesync
      requests, 17 matched, 369 replies discarded as 'Duplicate time
      message 0'. The client's in-game pings are not standalone 12-byte
      packets - vcncNet ATTACHES the 4-byte TIME section to outgoing DATA
      packets (inserted at wire 8-11, payload shifts to 12; client log
      proof: plain telemetry 'hdr size 8'/24B vs attached 'hdr size 12'/
      28B). The old sz==12 gate ignored EVERY in-game ping; clients got
      only the 1Hz idx-0 beacons and deduped them; drift accumulated ->
      dead reckoning degraded -> planes warp progressively = 'latency' that
      worsens over a session, on a clean wire. FIX: reply to ANY packet
      with data[2]&0x80 (build_time_reply already reads ti from offset 8 -
      correct for BOTH forms); composite packets fall through to normal
      processing. BONUS UNIFICATIONS: the attached TIME section IS the
      long-mysterious v340-v364 incrementing 4-byte 'counter prefix' (the
      >H at 8 is the TimeIndex - the unwrap had been stripping the ping and
      answering no one), and run_220823's 'ZERO 12-byte pings in-game'
      (v213) - they were attached all along.

v456f5 [NET/CRITICAL - THE COLLECTIVE CTD] 2026-08-22 stress test, ~28
      players: most of the room CTD'd simultaneously. Every crashed client
      log (messages36/39/67/85; 34 died to a secondary DirectSound buffer
      loss) ends on the SAME event: 'Create NetParachuter. St=26,
      ONumber=2050' -> 'Exception: bounds error / class ARR<char *,2048>
      [2050] 0..2047 / ring.hpp:27'.
      * ROOT CAUSE: the client's object table is a fixed 2048-entry array -
        ONumbers are hard-capped at 0x07FF. v441f5 made the allocator
        monotonic-only on the reasoning that the u16 space (0x0100..0xFFFF)
        'never wraps in a session'; correct for the wire, WRONG for the
        client. An evening of 28-player spawn/death/bail churn marched the
        counter past 2047; the first broadcast CREATE above the bound (a
        bail's parachuter at 2050) indexed out of bounds on every client
        that received it = the synchronized room-wide crash.
      * FIX: OBJ_NUMBER_MAX = 0x07FF. next_obj_number() rings within
        0x0100..0x07FF (1792 ids), skipping the in-use set gathered by
        _obj_numbers_in_use(): every session's live my_obj_number and
        para_obj_number, plus all _obj_number_history entries younger than
        OBJ_HISTORY_GRACE_SEC (300s) - preserving the v324/v441f5 wrong-
        plane protections across wraps. The set is snapshotted BEFORE
        _obj_num_lock (lock order: sl never nests inside it). Pathological
        full-ring exhaustion (~1800 live+recent objects) logs CRITICAL and
        issues anyway rather than deadlocking spawns.
      * Parachuter Numbers now also enter the owner's _obj_number_history
        at allocation - previously para_obj_number=None at landing made the
        id instantly re-issuable while peers could still hold the canopy.
      * OPEN from the same night: high-occupancy LAG reports - needs the
        box's PERF windows around peak (relay fanout at 28 players ~ 10k
        pps out); not yet investigated.

