v423f5: [SCENE] MAP DAMAGE DISPLAY POLARITY - the msg-69 per-scene byte is the DAMAGE
      percent, rendered by the client AS-IS. We were sending condition (100 = healthy), so
      every intact scene displayed '100% damage'. v296's 'condition' label was inferred,
      never checked against the binary - the same failure as v285's flag labels and v345's
      MEC nibbles.

      PROOF, both directions:
        * BINARY: msg-69 handler = FUN_004f5130 (VNET dispatch table 0xc81ed8 slot 69; base
          anchored by slot 88 -> 0xc82038 -> FUN_004f4120, the known msg-88 handler). It
          asserts (Length-1) % sizeof(SCENE_TAG_MESSAGE::PACKED_INFO) == 0 and hands the
          3-byte records to FUN_0048e9a0, which locates the scene node by id (node+0x4c) and
          writes THE RAW BYTE to node+0x30 (+ stamps node+0x20 with the time). The
          AIRFIELD_CONDITION_LIST_BOX detail line (FUN_006dcc60 @ 0x006dcd37) formats that
          value straight into "%s %i%%" (label = string-table 0x61a, value = dlg+0x13c). No
          inversion anywhere on the traced path.
        * LIVE, twice: v295 sent the supply average (~100 on full fields) -> '100% damage'
          everywhere; v296 sent condition=100 for pristine -> STILL '100% damage'
          everywhere. Two different ~100 payloads, identical wrong display; 0 is pristine.

      FIX: val = 100 - round(scene_efficiency*100), clamped 0..100. Nothing to 'reset'
      server-side - SCENE_HP was never populated (no damage occurred); the 100% was pure
      polarity. The unknown-scene filler record already sends 0, which is now correct by
      construction. Log tag flips hpNN -> dmgNN.

      FALSIFY: join the arena -> every scene reads 0% damage; `crumble` one scene -> its
      percentage rises while the others stay 0. REVERT: restore val = efficiency*100.

      BONUS RE (logged for later): the CargoRequest/delivery economy lives at FUN_00559250
      (Msn_Prod.cpp) - inbound 0x6b cargo request, outbound 0x76 (delivery record) and 0x7c
      (DELIV_BONUS_SCENE m/f/a + NotEnough + distance/time) - a decoded route into cargo
      resupply missions when we want them.

