v421f5: [OPS] MANUAL `restart` COMMAND - restart the running build with no git pull, from
      the server terminal OR the web console, without connecting to the VM.
      GAME SERVER: the restart half of perform_update (v329) is factored into _schedule_restart
      (honours UPDATE_RESTART_MODE: exit-and-let-supervisor / external command / none), so the
      new perform_restart reuses the EXACT proven mechanism and there is still ONE restart
      implementation. perform_restart takes the SAME _update_lock as perform_update, so a
      restart and an update - or two restarts - can never race (the second is refused with a
      log line). It announces to every player first (broadcast_everyone) and pauses 1.5s so the
      message is delivered before the process exits. New console command `restart` (terminal +
      web queue, same path as `update`); added to `help`.
      WEB: a grey 'Restart (no update)' button beside 'Update & Restart' on the live console,
      with a confirm dialog spelling out no-pull + players-disconnected; it queues cmd=restart
      through the existing /admin/console_cmd (which already forwards arbitrary console
      commands, so no endpoint change). Output streams under the RESTART tag.
      TESTED (stubbed exit): schedules and calls os._exit(0); the lock releases between calls
      so a second restart still works; a concurrent attempt while the lock is held is refused
      with no exit; players are warned first.
      SAFETY NOTE: like `update`, this relies on the supervisor's restart policy (systemd
      Restart=always etc.) - with UPDATE_RESTART_MODE='none' it does nothing but say so.
      FALSIFY: `restart` at the terminal (or the web button) drops the server and the
      supervisor brings it back logging the v421f5 banner within a few seconds; players get the
      'Server is restarting' notice; issuing `restart` during an `update` is refused. REVERT:
      delete perform_restart + the `restart` command + the web button/handler; inline
      _schedule_restart's body back into perform_update (or leave it - it's behaviour-neutral).

