v417f5: [NETCODE/CRITICAL] RELIABLE-SEND TIMEOUT NO LONGER ABANDONS THE SEQ - the true root
      cause of the '82 percent' hang (and, most likely, of the endemic one-way stall family).
      GHIDRA VERDICT ON v416f5's OPEN QUESTION: with FA.exe loaded, functions were created at
      0x4f4120 (msg-88 AceOrRankChangedCB) and its accessor 0x4f2560. The handler is a clean
      bounded 512-slot scan (mgr NULL-guarded, index-guarded) that on unknown PI logs and
      RETURNS - it cannot hang or crash a client in any state. The 82% screen is simply
      WaitingForStartPlaceList (strings 0xa31a10 / native timeout 0xa29f10) waiting for a
      grant that never arrived; the victims' silent final lines are unflushed log tail from
      the kill, not a death inside the compound.
      THE ACTUAL BUG: send_rel (v359f5) retransmits an un-ACKed reliable seq only until `to`
      (3-5s), then rme(seq) and GIVES UP - but nrel() already advanced, so the next sends go
      out as N+1, N+2, ... The client's reliable RX is strictly in-order (vcnc QRcv), so the
      abandoned seq N leaves a PERMANENT hole: the client holds every later reliable message
      while unreliable traffic keeps flowing. That is precisely the field picture - Warrior's
      client logged unreliable ACE88s to its last line while the granted msg-23 and the
      msg-96 answer (both sent instantly, per the run log) never surfaced; Snoman identical
      within 0.4s; fresh sessions (fresh seq space) on retry worked at once. v359f5's own
      comment documents the wedge mechanism; the `to` cap quietly reintroduced it through the
      back door, and no INFO-level TX logging existed to see the abandonment happen (the gap
      v412f5 flagged).
      FIX: on window expiry send_rel no longer rme()s the seq. A per-send background keeper
      (_rel_keeper, daemon thread) keeps the ACK event registered and re-sends the same pkt
      at REL_KEEPER_INTERVAL_S=1.0 until the ACK lands (the late copy un-wedges the client's
      QRcv, which drains its out-of-order queue - the v359f5 recovery, just never given up
      on), the session closes, or REL_KEEPER_MAX_S=120 expires. Every takeover and resolution
      logs at INFO under [RELKEEP], closing the send-side visibility gap in the same stroke.
      Caller semantics unchanged: blocking window and return value as before. Cost: at most
      1 pkt/s per held seq. KNOWN LIMIT (accepted): 512 further sends while a seq is held
      re-keys its event and that keeper runs to the cap - a session that deep into a wedged
      downlink is dead anyway.
      RELATION TO v416f5: the ACE88 gate stands as useful traffic reduction (no scoreboard
      pushes at clients mid-rebuild) but is no longer the load-bearing fix; the mechanism it
      guessed at is disproven and its own FALSIFY note is superseded by this entry.
      FALSIFY: grep [RELKEEP] - takeovers should appear occasionally and resolve 'ACKed after
      Xs'; a client frozen >5s during HQ->fly (alt-tab through the load) still receives its
      grant and spawns; the 82% both-at-once-fail/one-at-a-time-works pattern does not recur.
      If RELKEEP lines show 'GAVE UP' on LIVE clients (still sending), the ACK rx path is the
      next suspect (see the Moira SEQ9 bits8-16=2 anomaly). REVERT: restore the unconditional
      rme(seq)+log tail in send_rel and delete _rel_keeper + the two consts; no wire change.

