      the killer's respawns, bounded only by OBJ_HISTORY_GRACE_SEC after the Number retires.
      FALSIFY: shoot a plane down, respawn IMMEDIATELY, and let the victim's plane fall after
      your new spawn: the log shows 'attribution: EXACT/LATCHED ... [resolved via
      retired-Number history]' and the kill banner + score land on the shooter; grep for the
      reworded 'no session owns that object any more (current or retired)' line - it should
      now appear only for genuinely departed shooters. REVERT: delete the two history-fallback
      blocks in the attribution step (marked v415f5); no state or wire format changed.

v414f5: [NETCODE/BUGFIX] DEATHS EATEN AS CHAT - the named-chat (0xcd) dispatch tested
      pl[8]==0xcd WITHOUT the sub==0x00 scan-wrap guard every other pl[8] handler uses, so
      ANY message with 0xcd at offset 8 was consumed as chat. The long-form death
      delete-notify carries the HUNTER object u16 at pl[8:10] - so every victim of a plane
      whose object Number ends in 0xcd had their death eaten: no DEATH, no DELETE3 to peers,
      no kill credit, object never retired. Field case run_20260803_172220.log 15:21:59.330
      (messages64): Gary's death naming ALL41_MAD's 0x02cd logged as "[CHAT] [Gary]: '\x01'";
      MAD's client GC'd the frozen ghost 27.8s later ('plane disappeared, no kill or leave
      message'), the PEERDEL cleanup was swallowed as unowned, and MAD's own death 49s later
      could not resolve the latched 0x02ca -> 'no creditable shooter' -> peers saw a CRASH.
      Deterministic for the whole life of any 1-in-256 object Number; the collision-125
      NumberTo low byte is exposed to the same sniff. NOTE: handle_compound's inner test
      (inner[8]==0xcd, line ~9290) shares the flaw but has no field evidence yet - logged
      here, deliberately untouched (one fix per version).
      FIX: gate the pl[8] disjunct on sub==0x00, matching the house convention (chat 0x14,
      team 0x44, leave 0x40, delete 0x03 all do this). Chat is unaffected in both wire
      forms: direct named chat is sub==0xcd (second disjunct), prefixed named chat reaches
      the check with sub normalised to 0x00 by the v327 block. REPLAY-VERIFIED on the real
      captured bytes: Gary's eaten death now falls through to the death handler, MAD's
      processed death is unchanged, direct + prefixed named chat still dispatch to chat, a
      scan-wrapped delete stays out of chat (5/5).
      FALSIFY: engineer a hunter object with low byte 0xcd (or just play until one occurs):
      the victim's delete logs DEATH/KILL/DELETE3 with a '[CHAT]' line nowhere near it, and
      lobby/in-game named chat still broadcasts. REVERT: drop the sub==0x00 guard from the
      0xcd check (single line).

v413f5: [CONSOLE/FEATURE] `say <message>` - SERVER-WIDE ANNOUNCEMENT to every player, all
      arenas and the lobby at once (alias: `broadcast`; works from the terminal AND the web
      admin Live Console, which queues onto the same command path). Each client gets the
      chat form it can actually RENDER in its current state, both proven from the binary:
      IN-GAME sessions get msg-20 CHANNEL 4 - RE of the display handler FUN_004f6030 shows
      channel 4 is the client's built-in ANNOUNCE channel: it upper-cases the text, prints
      the chat line, and pushes the text as a ~10-second on-screen banner via FUN_0046a8a0
      (Msn_Serv.cpp mission-service message, duration 10000ms). The handler's first act is
      GetPlayer(PlayerIndex) and an unknown PI DROPS the line ('ChatMessage from unknown
      player'), so each recipient is stamped with its OWN index - self-resolution, the same
      property every reflected chat already relies on. The chat line reads 'TheirName: TEXT'
      (cosmetic; the banner shows the text alone). LOBBY sessions get the 0xcd 'Server'
      named chat (name inline, no PI - the ban-notice form).
      SAFETY: FUN_004f6030's channel-4 path strcpy's into a 512-byte stack buffer with no
      bounds check, so ANNOUNCE_MAX_CHARS=200 caps every send far below the client's buffer.
      Unit-verified with the real packet builders: in-game recipients get appspace msg-20
      [0x14][ch=4][own PI u32][squad u32][text 00], lobby recipients get [0xcd]'Server'00
      text00; a 500-char input truncates to 200 on every wire form; a blank sends nothing.
      FALSIFY: type `say Server restarting in 5 minutes` in the web console -> ANNOUNCE log
      line with the in-game/lobby split; every flying client shows the uppercased chat line
      + a 10s on-screen banner; every lobby client shows 'Server: ...' in chat.

v412f5: [NETCODE/BUGFIX] ONE-SIDED HIT REGISTRATION - damage-28 frames lost to two dispatch
      collisions (ALL41_MAD vs Gary head-to-head, messages55 / server_3_.log 15:54-15:58,
      room 53: MAD obj 0x0176, Gary 0x0170, Taurus@FA 0x0175). FIELD EVIDENCE: every relayed
      damage frame in the engagement is Gary's 19/28/46/55B forms; his three 37-BYTE bursts
      (15:55:04.374, 15:57:21.004, 15:58:29.033) are the ONLY sizes with no 'hit -> relay'
      line, and his two PREFIXED frames (15:57:21.512, 15:58:28.508, cmd=0x0542 sub=0x1c)
      died silently. The victims of those five frames were never told they were hit.
      TWO BUGS, both dispatch-order: (A) THE LENGTH-RULE COLLISION AGAIN (v392/v393's
      family): a damage frame is 10+9*count bytes, so a 3-record burst is exactly 37B ->
      body 33B -> T=0x12 by the appspace length rule -> it entered the tb==0x12 spawn/lobby
      block (which sits BEFORE the damage handler; the only tb block that does) and was
      swallowed by its catch-all. 3-record bursts are the signature of sustained fire -
      precisely the head-to-head scenario. (B) THE cmd GATE REGRESSED v333: the pl[8]==0x1c
      prefixed-damage fallback lives INSIDE `if cmd == 0:`, so prefixed damage was captured
      and never relayed - and the v352 'relayed with the prefix attached' mis-walk SUSPECT
      could never even occur through this path anymore, it simply dropped.
      FIX (two edits, same pattern as their parents): (1) `_dmg_1c` escape on the tb==0x12
      block, exactly mirroring v393's `_catalog_3a` - keys on the damage sub in both wire
      forms, so the real tb=0x12 out-4 spawn (sub=0x04), lobby traffic (0xe1/0xde/0xce...)
      and the hangar catalog keep their block; (2) 0x1c joins PREFIXED_REFRAME_SUBS, riding
      v411f5's cmd release: a prefixed damage frame re-frames to CLEAN direct bytes and
      takes the mature sub==0x1c path (victim DAMAGED mark, kill latch, relay of a frame
      whose header matches its length - retiring the v356 in-relay strip's prefixed case).
      Verified by REPLAYING all five real lost frames + six controls through the extracted
      reframe/escape/dispatch logic: 3x 37B bursts -> relay, 2x prefixed -> relay; 19/46B
      direct unchanged, spawn out-4 / lobby 0xe1 / 17B hangar catalog / prefixed spawn all
      keep their original dispatch.
      NOT EXPLAINED BY THIS FIX, logged for next session: MAD's client sent ZERO out-28s all
      session (his own hit detection found nothing - client-side aim/interp, not a server
      relay loss; his collision detect at 15:58:37 proves proximity rendering worked), and
      the 'ERROR: Unsupported message 8' clusters on MAD's client (15:55:26 x3, 15:55:54 x2,
      15:57:38-40 x7, ~280ms apart = reliable-resend cadence, correlated with Taurus's
      damage/death) - the server's sends are not logged at INFO, so if this recurs on v412
      the next step is send-side instrumentation, not guessing.
      FALSIFY: a sustained-fire dogfight logs 'hit -> relay' lines for 37B frames (grep
      'relaying a 37B damage frame' / '(37B)'), and a shooter on a wrapped connection still
      registers hits on the victim (REFRAME 'damage 28 ... releasing cmd' followed by
      'hit -> relay' with the 4B-shorter clean length).

v411f5: [NETCODE/BUGFIX] RE-FRAMED PREFIXED MESSAGES ACTUALLY DISPATCH - fixes the
      parachute-landing CTD (DBD_GCEXTREME, messages09 / server_3_.log 23:15). ROOT CAUSE,
      one flaw with two faces: the entire in-game dispatcher (delete-notify death path,
      DAMAGE28/collision relays, out-4 spawn/bail handlers, echo logic) is gated on
      `if cmd == 0:`, and the v246/v340 re-frame recovered a prefixed frame's bytes but LEFT
      cmd AT ITS PREFIXED VALUE - so every re-framed message was captured, logged, and then
      refused by the very dispatcher it was recovered for. Three casualties in one minute of
      server_3_.log: 23:15:14 a re-framed collision 125 never relayed; 23:15:42 the BAIL
      out-4 lost; 23:15:49 the re-framed bail delete-notify produced no death and no husk
      removal (peers GC'd the ghost 0x01a2 themselves 20s later, the PEERDEL cluster).
      The bail additionally slipped EVERY net: its 15-byte parachute form carries inner type
      0xf2 (the appspace type byte encodes size), so the pl[8]==0x04 scan fallback - which
      requires the 33-byte spawn shape pl[5]==0x12 - never matched, and sub 0x04 was not in
      PREFIXED_REFRAME_SUBS. CONSEQUENCE CHAIN, fully evidenced: no parachuter object, no
      ServerConfirm -> the client's chute kept NumberVar=-1 -> on canopy touchdown the
      teardown ran 'DelObject -1, bsr=0' -> null lookup -> Access violation v.addr:0x10 at
      Main.cpp:2407 (the exact messages09 crash signature). The lost bail also leaves the
      server's object-Number counter one behind the client's (the chute consumed one
      client-side), which is the documented 'Confirm object N not found in list' dead-engine
      follow-on - so this wrap-state bail loss was lethal twice over.
      FIX (two coordinated edits, one root cause): (1) PREFIXED_REFRAME_RELEASES_CMD - after
      a full re-frame the frame is BYTE-IDENTICAL to a direct arrival, so cmd is released to
      0 and the frame takes the direct dispatch path. Provably safe: it cannot re-enter the
      v364f5 counter-unwrap (a re-framed frame is self-consistent as direct, which the
      unwrap's first condition excludes for bc==0; bc>0 frames fail its pl[0]==0 test), and
      byte-identical input + identical variables = the exact path the direct form has taken
      all along. (2) 0x04 joins PREFIXED_REFRAME_SUBS, routing a prefixed bail to the mature
      sub==0x04 parachute consumer (Number consumed, ident resynced, confirm + peer create +
      descent delete - the path that served the FIRST bail of the night flawlessly) and a
      prefixed 33B spawn to the direct tb==0x12 confirm instead of the scan shim.
      Verified by REPLAYING the three real casualty payloads from server_3_.log through the
      extracted reframe+gate+dispatch logic: wrapped bail -> parachute consumer, wrapped
      delete -> death path, wrapped collision -> relay; direct forms and a prefixed
      non-set sub (0x22) byte-for-byte unchanged; the counter-unwrap never re-fires.
      Single-variable revert: PREFIXED_REFRAME_RELEASES_CMD=False + drop 0x04 from the set.
      FALSIFY: bail out twice in one internet session (the wrap state follows the first
      cycle) -> second bail logs '[PARA] BAILED OUT' + a REFRAME line ending 'releasing cmd
      for direct dispatch', the client gets its '(p)' ServerConfirm, the chute lands with NO
      crash, and the bail delete books the DEATH + husk removal on peers (no PEERDEL
      GC-cluster for that object).

v410f5: [WEB] PILOT EDITOR EDITS THE PER-MODE LADDER BOARDS. Follow-through on the v409f5
      finding that the manual pilot-editor test could not touch the boards: /admin/edit_pilot
      gains a 'Ladder Boards - FFA / TC / Events' fieldset exposing all twelve per-mode
      columns (fighter score, bomber score, kills, deaths per board) with the same bounds as
      their career counterparts, so an admin can seed a board (e.g. copy a career total into
      the board it was really earned on) or correct one after the fact. The game client never
      sees these columns; RANK still derives from the career scores only. Both the form and
      the POST reuse the v240 guards - a field is rendered and saved ONLY when its column
      exists in the DB and was actually posted - so an un-migrated DB shows a shorter form
      and saves cleanly. server.py change: VERSION bump only.
      Unit-verified END-TO-END on real sqlite DBs, running the exact GET (have-filter +
      COALESCE select) and POST (_clamp + partial-update SQL) logic: on a migrated DB all 15
      fields of the test subset render, board values round-trip (1234/4567/16/99), an
      over-range kills value clamps to 65535 and junk input defaults to 0 without touching
      neighbours; on an un-migrated DB the board fields are hidden and a POST carrying board
      keys saves nothing.
      FALSIFY: open AC2E_Bigalon in the editor after restart -> the new fieldset shows twelve
      zeroed board fields; set FFA kills=16/deaths=99 and save -> the ladder's FFA tab shows
      them immediately while Career is untouched; on a DB missing the columns the fieldset
      simply doesn't render.

v409f5: [SCORING/BUGFIX] CLEAN CRASHES REGISTER AS DEATHS AGAIN - the wire tells a crash from
      an exit after all. Field report (run_20260803_224958 / messages23): an undamaged
      fly-into-the-ground produced NO death at all, and every per-mode ladder board stayed
      empty. ONE root cause: v400f5's exit-crash gate, inherited from v232's 'a crash and a
      clean ground exit are identical on the wire' finding, swallowed EVERY undamaged airborne
      removal when the arena rule is OFF - and since no death ever fired, score_on_death never
      ran, so the v406-v408 mode boards had nothing to write (the manual pilot-editor test
      only writes CAREER columns; the boards fill from live scoring only - working as
      designed, starved by this bug).
      THE v232 PREMISE IS FALSIFIED by tonight's log: a ground impact reports exit 0x11
      (MEC=1, EEC=1) and is immediately followed by a StartPlace respawn request (one even
      RACED the delete by 3ms), while a voluntary exit reports 0xa0 (MEC=0xa, EEC=0) and is
      followed by the HQ screen - three of each in one session, perfectly separated. FIX: a
      MOVING removal with MEC=1 is a CRASH and books the death REGARDLESS of the arena's
      exit-crash rule (that rule now governs voluntary MEC=0xa exits only, exactly v400f5's
      intent). The land-and-swap case (also 0x11, the reason for the old caution) stays clean
      via the existing movement test - a parked plane reports movement 0.
      Verified by REPLAYING all six exits from tonight's log through the new predicate:
      3/3 crashes (movement 122k-347k, 0x11) -> deaths; 3/3 menu exits (0xa0, incl. the
      parked one) -> clean; damaged-exit and exit-crash-arena behaviour byte-identical to
      v400f5. The crash death flows through score_on_death -> career deaths/planes-lost AND
      the arena mode's deaths_<mode>/loss, so the ladder boards start filling.
      FALSIFY: fly into the ground undamaged in a Dogfighting arena -> DEATH 'CRASHED' log
      line, career deaths +1, FFA board deaths +1 and the plane's FFA cost deducted; exit to
      menu mid-flight -> still 'clean exit, NOT a death'; land, park, swap planes -> clean.

v408f5: [SCORING/WEB] LADDER RESTRUCTURED - FFA | TC | EVENTS ARE THE MAIN TABS, WITH THE
      FIGHTER/BOMBER SPLIT INSIDE EVERY BOARD. User direction: fighter/bomber should not be
      tabs of their own; the arena modes are the boards, and each carries the same
      fighter/bomber score split the game itself keeps.
      (1) Three more pilots columns - bscore_ffa/tc/events (bomber-flown points per mode).
      score_<mode> is REDEFINED as the mode's FIGHTER-flown points - safe because none of the
      v406/v407 columns has ever existed in a live DB (no restart since v400f5), so there is
      no data to migrate, only meaning to fix before first boot.
      (2) db_apply_score_delta: the per-mode tally now honours `bomber` exactly like the
      career does - bomber -> 'b' + score_<mode>. One rule, both ladders.
      (3) /ladder: FOUR tabs - Career | FFA | TC | Events. Career is the record the client
      itself renders (career fighter/bomber/total + career kills/deaths); each mode tab is a
      full 9-column scoreboard: #, Pilot, Rank, Fighter Score, Bomber Score, Total (bold),
      Kills, Deaths, K/D - all per-mode, sorted by that board's Total then its K/D, every
      header re-sortable. Old localStorage tab ids simply no-select -> Career default.
      Unit-verified: sqlite full-cycle with the real db_apply_score_delta - a fighter+bomber
      FFA session, a TC bomber kill and an Events fighter loss land as career (0f-after-clamp,
      1100b), FFA (500f, 700b), TC (0f, 400b), Events (0/0 - the loss clamped at 0), proving
      every column clamps independently and career/boards agree; the 4x9 page builder executed
      with two synthetic pilots shows independent per-board ordering (TC leader differs) and
      exactly 18 cells per 2-row board. (Two ASSERTION bugs during testing - a mispredicted
      clamp and a substring-count overlap - were in the TESTS, verified against the DB rows;
      the code was right both times.)
      FALSIFY: a pilot who flies a bomber in TC and a fighter in FFA shows points in exactly
      bscore_tc and score_ffa; the TC tab's Fighter Score column stays 0 for them; the Career
      tab equals the in-game HQ screen.

v407f5: [SCORING/WEB] EVERY BOARD IS A COMPLETE SCOREBOARD. Reported: the v406f5 FFA/TC/
      Events tabs still showed the CAREER kills/deaths on every board - only score was
      per-mode, so K/D and the kill columns were identical across arena types. Now each mode
      carries its full stat set:
      (1) Six more pilots columns - kills_ffa/tc/events + deaths_ffa/tc/events (same startup
      migration loop, default 0).
      (2) db_credit_kill(mode=): the killer's kills_<mode> and the victim's deaths_<mode>
      increment alongside the career counters; both score_on_death call sites (credited kill
      AND the no-shooter/AA death) pass mode, so a flak death in a TC arena lands in deaths_tc
      exactly like a player kill would. Career kills/deaths/streak/planes-lost semantics are
      byte-identical to before - the per-mode counters are strictly additive columns in the
      same UPDATE.
      (3) /ladder: the FFA/TC/Events boards now render their OWN kills, deaths and a K/D
      computed from them; the three career boards (Combined/Fighter/Bomber) keep the career
      kill/death columns - the DB has no per-airframe kill split and inventing one would lie.
      Each mode board also sorts by (mode score, mode K/D), so tie-breaks are per-mode too.
      Unit-verified FULL-CYCLE on a real sqlite DB with the actual db_credit_kill +
      db_apply_score_delta: Hunter 2xFFA + 1xTC kills -> career kills 3 with boards 2/1/0 and
      scores 1000/500; Prey's four deaths (3 shot down + 1 AA in Events) -> career 4 with
      boards 2/1/1 and planes_lost_ai fed; per-mode scores clamp at 0 independently; the
      career columns are untouched by any board write.
      FALSIFY: two pilots duel in a Dogfighting arena then move to a Territorial Combat arena
      - after one kill in each, the FFA tab shows 1/0 and the TC tab shows 1/0 independently
      while Combined shows 2 kills; an AA death in an Events arena bumps the Events tab's
      Deaths column only.

v406f5: [SCORING/WEB] PER-MODE SCOREBOARDS - FFA | TC | EVENTS LADDER TABS. The v405f5 tabs
      covered the two career columns; the user wants boards for the NEW scoring system's arena
      types too. That needs per-mode TALLIES, not just display - and it surfaced a design gap:
      'Events' lost its identity inside scoring_mode_for_room (v402f5 resolved it to a value
      column immediately), so nothing downstream could ever know a death happened in an Events
      arena. Changes:
      (1) 'events' is now a FIRST-CLASS scoring mode: scoring_mode_for_room returns
      'ffa'|'tc'|'events'|None (category 'event*' -> 'events'; the web override accepts
      'events' too - new 'Force Events board' option in the arena editor dropdown). WHICH
      value column an Events arena prices planes with moved into plane_value: mode 'events'
      -> the EVENTS_VALUE_TABLE column (still the one-word flip, verified both ways).
      (2) pilots gains score_ffa / score_tc / score_events INTEGER DEFAULT 0 (startup
      migration, same pattern as the msg-25 columns; existing pilots start every board at 0).
      (3) db_apply_score_delta(mode=): the same delta is MIRRORED into the mode's tally
      column with the same never-negative clamp; career score/bomber_score and RANK are
      untouched by it - they remain the single ladder the client renders. All three
      score_on_death call sites (killer credit + both victim-loss branches) pass mode.
      (4) /ladder: six boards - Combined | Fighter | Bomber | FFA | TC | Events - same
      search/sort/persistence chrome, columns guarded for a pre-migration DB.
      Unit-verified END-TO-END incl. a REAL sqlite DB: category 'Events' and forced-'events'
      both resolve; events pricing follows EVENTS_VALUE_TABLE ('ffa'->111/'tc'->999 fixture);
      a scripted session (2 FFA kills, a TC loss, a bomber Events kill, a mode-less delta)
      leaves career=(650 fighter, 700 bomber) with boards (ffa 1000, tc clamped to 0,
      events 700) and the mode-less delta touching no board; 6-board orderings independent.
      FALSIFY: boot log prints the three pilots-column migrations once; a kill in a
      'Dogfighting' arena raises the pilot's FFA board only (career unchanged in the other
      boards); an 'Events'-category death logs the EVENTS_VALUE_TABLE plane cost and feeds
      score_events; the ladder's TC tab re-orders independently of Fighter/Bomber.

v405f5: [WEB] LADDER TABS - COMBINED | FIGHTER SCORE | BOMBER SCORE. The ladder only read the
      `score` column (Fighter Score, msg-25 f9) while FA keeps TWO boards: `bomber_score`
      (f10) is fed by what the pilot was FLYING when the points were earned (v242 rule), so a
      bomber pilot's career was invisible on the web. Now three tabs, each a fully sorted
      board: Combined (fighter+bomber - the total RANK is computed from, per
      db_apply_score_delta, so it is the canonical board and the default tab), Fighter, and
      Bomber. The Rank column shows the same combined-ladder rank on every tab - it IS the
      pilot's rank; a per-column rank would be an invention. One search box filters all three
      boards at once; the chosen tab persists across visits (localStorage); the column sorter
      is generalized to sortLadder(tableId, col). bomber_score is column-guarded so an
      un-migrated DB renders zeros instead of erroring. Bonus fix: pilot names in ladder rows
      are now HTML-escaped - they never were, and v317 explicitly allows names containing
      < > & " ' (any printable ASCII), so a hostile pilot name could inject markup into the
      public ladder page. Tab chrome lives in LADDER_TABS_ASSETS, a plain string, for the
      same f-string brace-escaping reason as ADMIN_TABS_ASSETS.
      Unit-verified end-to-end with three synthetic pilots: per-board ordering differs
      correctly (bomber-heavy pilot tops Bomber and Combined but bottoms Fighter), rank is
      the combined-ladder rank everywhere, a '<'-bearing name renders escaped, and exactly
      one panel is visible in the emitted HTML.
      FALSIFY: /ladder shows three tabs; a pilot with fighter=200/bomber=4000 leads the
      Bomber board, trails the Fighter board, and holds the rank of their 4200 total on both;
      searching filters whichever tab is open; the tab choice survives a page reload.

v404f5: [WEB/SCORING] SCORING TABLES ON THE WEB + PER-ARENA SCORING CONTROL. Two user asks:
      the web server displays both value tables, and scoring/ranks are controllable per arena
      type. (1) /scoring - a Scoring Reference page linked from the dashboard nav and the
