      arena so it plays pure fighter-vs-fighter; fighter-bombers on fighter airframes (Bf-109
      /B variants, Typhoon, Hurr-IID...) stay, per the BOMBER_PLANE_IDS classification the
      v242 scoring work already established (41 slots hidden, 80 kept).
      WHY SERVER-SIDE: definitive Ghidra pass (2026-08-02, FA.exe) proved no native per-era or
      per-class hangar restriction exists. Hangar gate FUN_006de570 admits a plane EITHER via
      free-selection ((MyRights & DAT_00bf53ec) != 0 && !(PLN_DEF+0xb & 1)) OR via the byte-0
      camp mask (FUN_00438330); PLN_DEF+0xb is a static .pli byte and the gamedef Year/Month
      fields (0x4d8/0x4d9) are parsed but read by NOTHING in the hangar path. Byte-0 is the
      only server lever, and MyRights is only sent non-zero to moderators, so the free-
      selection bypass is inert for players (a moderator may still see the full hangar in a
      filtered arena - existing staff behaviour, not a leak).
      MECHANISM: identical to (and independent from) the war-date filter. New function
      _apply_fighters_only_filter chained after _apply_war_date_filter at both decision
      points (plane_camp_for_room -> GAME_DEF byte0 write; _session_slot_camp -> 0x3a catalog
      echo), remapping BOMBER_PLANE_IDS slots to camp 7 -> apply_plane_teams writes byte0 0x80
      (hidden from every standard side, NEVER 0x00, so the GameDef.cpp:1902 CTD class is
      structurally impossible). The two filters are order-independent (both only remap TO 7).
      Opt-in via settings_json fighters_only=1; web_server.py gained the checkbox (GET render
      + POST persist, mirroring war_enabled). DB read fails OPEN (all planes shown).
      Verified offline: no-op without opt-in, no input mutation, Me-163B stays NU, Typhoon/
      Bf-109E-4/B/P-51D stay on their sides, B-17G/IL-2/C-47A remap to 7; compile OK both files.
      FALSIFY (one throwaway arena first): (1) ticked arena's hangar shows no bombers/attackers/
      transports on ANY side; (2) untouched arenas unchanged; (3) no 1902 CTD; (4) side-switch
      does not hang the HQ menu; (5) combines with war date (tick both on a 1941 arena).
      REVERT: FIGHTERS_ONLY_ENABLED = False (single flag, war-date filter unaffected).

v390f5: [INTERNET/CRITICAL] Fix the '100%% loading -> blank splash -> closes' hang for clients on
      a high-latency (internet) link. This is the real KILO/bigalon bug - NOT load, NOT the buffer.
      server.py only, one-line gate change.

      DIAGNOSIS (run_20260802_190259 + bigalon game log messages06 + vcncNet log): server now runs
      on GCP (34.72.115.211, ~200ms RTT), not LAN. bigalon connected, auth'd (Auth ACKed T+1.469s
      @19:04:16), then got a DEAD 15s gap and its pilot list was NEVER sent - AC2E_Bigalon appears
      exactly once in the whole server log (the startup DB dump), never in the session. The client
      sat on the splash and issued a clean vcncDisconnect @19:04:49. Other (slower-connecting)
      clients that session got their pilot lists fine.

      ROOT CAUSE: a race exposed only by latency. login() runs in a thread; it send_rel's the auth
      reply and BLOCKS a full RTT waiting for the ACK, and only THEN sets s.auth_done=True. The
      client fires its pilot-list request (reliable cv=512) the instant it receives the auth reply
      - i.e. ~0.5 RTT before the server's send_rel returns. The reliable-command dispatch was
      'if cv==4: start login; elif s.auth_done: queue; return' with NO else - so a cv=512 arriving
      in that ~1-RTT window while auth_done was still False was ACKed but SILENTLY DROPPED. No
      pilot list is ever sent, the client's lobby never populates, blank splash, timeout, close.
      On LAN the RTT is sub-millisecond so auth_done flips before the request arrives and the
      window doesn't exist - which is precisely why this never reproduced locally and only bit once
      the server moved to the internet.

      FIX: gate becomes 'elif s.auth_done or s._login_started'. _login_started is set the moment
      cv==4 is seen, so from the start of auth any non-cv4 reliable command is QUEUED into
      post_auth_cmds instead of dropped. The dispatch loop runs inside login() AFTER auth_done, so
      the queued command is still handled strictly post-auth (handle_post_auth unchanged) - we only
      stop throwing it away. A stray reliable packet arriving before cv==4 is ever seen is still
      dropped (login not started), so nothing pre-auth is processed.

      TEST: internet clients - especially fast-connecting / warm-cache ones - should now reach the
      pilot-selection screen and load in every time instead of hanging on the splash. Watch for the
      PILOTS line for each account right after its 'Auth ACKed'. REVERT: restore 'elif s.auth_done:'.

==============================================================================

v389f5: [PERF/SCALE] Move the telemetry-relay sendto() off the single RX thread - the throughput
      ceiling behind the load-related faults (KILO's dropped auth, and consistent with Lufty's
      state desync + ALL41_MAD's freeze). server.py only. Step toward the 50-player goal.

      THE CEILING: relay_telemetry builds one CUSTOMISED packet per flying peer (recipient tick
      re-stamp + per-peer _relay_seq) and did sock.sendto() for each one INLINE on the recv thread
      - O(N) syscalls per frame, O(N^2)/s across a room. Broadcasts (send pool), ACKs (async
      sender) and lazy object-creates were already off the RX thread; this highest-frequency path
      was not. While the RX thread is buried in that syscall storm it isn't calling recvfrom(),
      the OS buffer overflows, and inbound datagrams are dropped - v388 enlarged the buffer to
      absorb bursts, this removes the sustained per-frame cost so it scales.

      FIX: the RX thread still BUILDS every per-peer packet (userspace only; ALL shared per-peer
      relay state - _created_peers, _relay_seq, last_telem_tick - stays on the RX thread, so NO new
      cross-thread races are introduced) and appends (addr, pkt) to a batch. After the peer loop it
      hands the whole batch to ONE dedicated relay-send thread (_relay_send_loop) that performs the
      sendto() syscalls. One thread draining a FIFO queue of per-frame batches preserves per-peer
      ordering EXACTLY: each peer's _relay_seq is still strictly increasing and its packets still
      leave in that order (verified in isolation: 200 frames x 5 peers, every peer in-order).

      WHY IT'S SAFE: the offload is send-only and order-preserving; nothing about parsing,
      state, dedupe or create logic changes. RELAY_SEND_ASYNC=False restores the verbatim inline
      sendto for instant A/B comparison.

      REMAINING CEILING (for >50): the RX thread still PARSES every packet and BUILDS the O(N)
      per-peer packets serially. If that becomes the limit, the next step is a sharded RX-worker
      model (recv thread -> per-session-hashed ordered worker queues) so parsing/build parallelise
      too - bigger and race-sensitive, deferred until measurement shows it's needed.

      TEST: populated session - watch that a late joiner during a fight reaches '== v197 AUTH ==',
      that STALL-WATCH 'reliable RX idle' warnings drop sharply, and that planes still move
      smoothly (relay ordering intact). Startup shows a 'relay-send' thread. If anything looks off
      (stutter, dupes, lost objects), set RELAY_SEND_ASYNC=False and restart to compare.

      REVERT: RELAY_SEND_ASYNC=False (single-flag).

==============================================================================

v388f5: [PERF] Enlarge OS socket buffers so a new client's auth isn't dropped under load.
      server.py only. Fixes KILO's 'loads 100% -> blank splash -> awaiting-server-connection
      timer -> closes, never gets in'.

      DIAGNOSIS (run_20260801_023556 + KILO client log messages23): KILO connected during a
      10-player dogfight. SYN was handled (sid=116 'Ready') - SYNs run on their own thread - but
      the client's reliable AUTH DATA (client logged 'UserName KILO' / 'out 222' at 18:19:08)
      NEVER reached the server: there is no 'SEQ9 first reliable DATA' and no '== v197 AUTH ==' for
      that session. The client sat on a blank splash ~44s and closed (client log ends 18:19:52).
      A quieter retry 4 min later (sid=117) auth'd and got in fine. Meanwhile the log carried 228
      STALL-WATCH 'reliable RX idle' warnings (up to 65s) during the fight.

      ROOT CAUSE: the recv loop dispatches SYNs to threads but runs on_pkt for EVERY other packet
      (telemetry relay, ACKs, reliable auth/command DATA) SYNCHRONOUSLY on the single recv thread.
      While it is busy relaying a dogfight to ~10 peers it is not calling recvfrom(), and the
      default Windows UDP receive buffer (~64KB) overflows during the burst - so the kernel drops
      inbound datagrams, KILO's auth among them. Nothing in the server even sees the packet, which
      is why it left no trace.

      FIX: request SO_RCVBUF and SO_SNDBUF of 4 MB each right after socket creation (logged with the
      value the kernel actually grants - Windows typically honors it, Linux doubles it). ~64x the
      default buffer holds the inbound burst until the recv thread loops back to recvfrom, so a new
      client's auth (and existing players' reliable packets) survive a busy fight. SNDBUF is raised
      too so the relay's own sendto bursts don't stall.

      SCOPE / FOLLOW-UP: this absorbs BURSTS, which is the KILO case. It does not raise steady-state
      throughput - the synchronous single-thread relay is still the ceiling (~10-12 players is the
      edge; the 228 stall warnings reflect that pressure). If populated sessions keep growing, the
      next step is to decouple recvfrom from processing (fast recv thread -> per-session ordered
      worker queues) so relay work never blocks the socket drain. Deferred: bigger, concurrency-
      sensitive change; the buffer bump is the safe high-value fix for the reported symptom.

      TEST: at the next populated session, a late joiner during a fight should now reach
      '== v197 AUTH ==' and load in instead of hanging on the splash. Startup logs the granted
      SO_RCVBUF/SO_SNDBUF. REVERT: delete the setsockopt loop.

==============================================================================

v387f5: [SESSION] Dead-client idle reaper - the last uncovered exit path. server.py only.
      Completes the session-cleanup set alongside v385f5 (reconnect-reap) and v386f5 (clean
      disconnect). This one covers an ungraceful CTD whose client NEVER reconnects: no
      vcncDisconnect is sent (so v386f5 can't fire) and the account never comes back (so the
      v385f5 reconnect-reap can't fire), leaving a single lingering ghost until a server restart.

      WHY 60s IS SAFE (not a live-player killer): a live client streams two-way TIME pings
      (clock-sync RESYNC, ~5s cadence - the game needs continuous sync for telemetry timing) plus
      reliable ACKs the whole time. DEBUG capture run_20260801_002705 measured max inbound gap =
      8.3s over a 40s session, median 0.01s. So a session with ZERO inbound for 60s is provably a
      dead client, not an idle one. Earlier worry about a 226s 'silence' (server_2_.log, Warrior)
      was an artifact of INFO-level logging hiding the sz=8/sz=12 ping/ACK traffic - those packets
      were flowing; they're just DEBUG-only.

      MECHANISM: on_pkt stamps s.last_rx on EVERY inbound packet that resolves to a session (so
      time-pings/ACKs keep a live session fresh - it can't be clipped). A daemon reaper scans every
      IDLE_REAP_SCAN_S (15s) and, for each AUTH-established, not-already-torn session whose last
      inbound is older than IDLE_REAP_S (60s), calls _teardown_session (same cleanup as every other
      exit: broadcast the pilot delete, free ClientNumber + start-place, pop). Mid-auth sessions are
      excluded (they can be briefly quiet during the ~15s handshake). Reaping a dead in-game
      session is desirable - it broadcasts the leave so peers drop the stale plane.

      FLAGS: IDLE_SESSION_REAP=True (set False to disable), IDLE_REAP_S=60.0, IDLE_REAP_SCAN_S=15.0.

      TEST: connect a client, hard-kill it (Task Manager / pull the plug - NO clean quit), do NOT
      reconnect. Within ~60-75s expect 'IDLE-REAP  reaped idle session ... no inbound for Ns
      (ungraceful CTD, no reconnect)' and active_sessions back to baseline. Regression check: a
      normally-connected idle client sitting in the lobby/arena for minutes must NOT be reaped.

      REVERT: IDLE_SESSION_REAP=False (single-flag).

==============================================================================

v386f5: [SESSION] Clean disconnect now actually closes the session (no more ghost after a
      graceful client Exit/Quit). server.py only. Builds on v385f5's _teardown_session.

      RE of vcncNet.dll (loaded into Ghidra) settled what the client does on a clean close
      (FUN_007e71b0): a three-step handshake ExitAppSpace -> Disconnect -> Close, each blocking
      on a server reply. vcncDisconnect (@0x1000b43c) sends reliable command 2 and treats a reply
      whose command field == 0x64 as success (else / on timeout it logs 'Can't disconnect
      server'). Our pl2[3]=0x64 IS that ack. On the wire the disconnect is a normal reliable
      packet (command 2 at pl[2:4]) that arrives via the compound path (handle_compound ->
      inner_cmd==2), so the server already parsed and answered it correctly.

      THE BUG: the inner_cmd==2 handler sent the 0x64 reply and returned WITHOUT tearing the
      session down. DEBUG capture run_20260801_002705 shows it exactly: client sends ExitAppSpace
      then Disconnect, server replies (seq=25 'disconnect reply', ACKed OK), client pings a few
      more seconds and vanishes (10054) - and the session is STILL live afterwards. Ghost.
      (Earlier confusion: 'disconnect reply'/cmd==2 showed 0 in older logs because those sessions
      were all ungraceful window-kills that never sent vcncDisconnect; and one client log showed
      VCNCMD_FATALLOSTCONNECTION *before* the disconnect - that was the pre-v385f5 ghost-lockout
      window, where the connection was already dead so vcncDisconnect never transmitted.)

      FIX: both disconnect handlers (compound inner_cmd==2, and the non-compound post-auth cmd==2
      fallback) now send the 0x64 ack and THEN call _teardown_session(why='(client
      vcncDisconnect)'). send_rel is synchronous, so the client gets its ack before the session is
      torn down; the teardown broadcasts the pilot delete and frees the ClientNumber/slot. Result:
      a graceful Exit/Quit closes the session immediately and cleanly - the reconnect-reap
      (v385f5) remains the backstop only for ungraceful CTDs that never send a disconnect.

      NOTE: this does NOT tear down on ExitAppSpace (inner_cmd==5) - leaving the app space can be
      a return-to-menu while staying connected; only the explicit Disconnect closes the session.

      TEST: DEBUG on, clean menu-quit; expect 'inner vcncDisconnect -> disconnect reply +
      teardown' then the session gone (no lingering 10054 ghost, active_sessions back to baseline).

      REVERT: drop the two _teardown_session(why='(client vcncDisconnect)') calls.

