v356: [WIRE/CRITICAL] the malformed damage frames are PREFIXED frames relayed with the prefix
      still attached. Strip it instead of dropping - this is why hits did not register.

      v355 refused to relay any damage frame failing the 10+count*9 structure test. That stopped
      the CTD but THREW THE DAMAGE AWAY, which is exactly the other half of the reports:
      "I was shooting Moira, no hits reported, no damage to Moira", "I stopped taking damage and
      continued to fly", "he took his wing off and had a collision detected, Warrior did not".

      server_11_ logged 15 MISMATCH frames across 6 pilots (ALL41_MAD, Moira@HQ, Taurus@FA,
      Warrior, DBD_GCEXTREME, Snoman). EVERY ONE is a prefixed frame; the real, perfectly
      well-formed frame starts at offset 4:
          0011 0000 | 00f200001c...  23B = 4 + 19   count 1
          0024 0000 | 018200001c...  32B = 4 + 28   count 2
          000b 0000 | 021200001c...  41B = 4 + 37   count 3
          0013 0000 | 02a200001c...  50B = 4 + 46   count 4
          0003 0000 | 03c200001c...  68B = 4 + 64   count 6   <- the CTD frame, exactly
      All five distinct shapes verified: FAIL as-is, PASS stripped, inner length == 10+count*9.

      MECHANISM: v327 normalises the sub on prefixed messages but deliberately leaves the bytes
      untouched, and this relay forwards `stored` verbatim. Peers therefore received a frame 4
      bytes longer than its own header declares, desynced their record walk, and died on
      `Length=-19` at Engine\Net\Network.cpp:249 - or on a wild pointer at FA30\Main.cpp:2407
      when the garbage was dereferenced before any bounds check caught it.

      FIX: if the frame fails the structure test, retry it at offset 4. If that passes, relay
      the stripped frame (logged once per session). Only if BOTH fail is it dropped.
      This should close the CTD and restore the missing damage in one change.

      STRUCTURE CONFIRMED FROM BOTH ENDS - worth keeping. Client-side `in 28'N` sizes across ten
      player logs are exactly 6 + count*9 (15,24,33,42,60,69,78,87,96), which is our
      10 + count*9 minus the 4-byte [bc][T][00][00] header. A malformed 68B frame would appear
      client-side as `in 28'64`, which is absent from that sequence.

      PROCESS: v352 raised prefix-carryover as an explicit SUSPECT and refused to act on it
      because the observed overrun was 19 rather than 4. That was the right call - the overrun
      is a consequence of the desynced walk, not the size of the prefix - and one more
      instrumented run turned the suspect into a measurement across 15 samples and 6 pilots.

