v345: [SCORING] deliberate crashes were not counted as deaths. The MEC nibble table had the
      crash and the menu-exit the wrong way round.

      SYMPTOM: pilot crashes repeatedly, HUD death count never moves. In the 07:32 session
      AC2E_Bigalon spawned SIX times and lostpilots stayed at 8 for five of them, incrementing
      to 9 exactly once.

      ROOT CAUSE: is_death = mec_nib in DEATH_MEC_NIBBLES, and the set was {0xa, 0x5, 0x6}.
          deliberate crash  -> exit=0x11 -> nibble 1  -> NOT in set -> "re-fly/plane-swap"
          exit to menu      -> exit=0xa0 -> nibble 0xa -> in set     -> counted as a death
      Exactly backwards. The pilot confirmed all five were flown into the ground on purpose,
      and that the only removal which ever scored was the menu exit.
      The v232 comment asserting "A CRASH ... MissExitCode 26" is WRONG - 26 (nibble 0xa) is the
      MENU EXIT. That comment is left in the file, contradicted in place, so the next reader
      does not re-derive it and re-introduce the bug.

      FIX: add 0x1 to DEATH_MEC_NIBBLES. Deliberately NOT removing 0xa - leaving the game while
      airborne should still cost the plane, and that is the existing anti-abuse behaviour.
      SAFETY: adding 0x1 routes crashes into the `elif is_death` branch, which still applies the
      existing "exited UNDAMAGED and PARKED -> clean exit, NOT a death" movement test. So if a
      genuine land-and-swap also reports 0x11 it is still excluded, by the check that branch was
      built for. No new discriminator was invented.

      IF THIS DOES NOT FIX IT: the crashes will now fall to that second gate instead of the
      first, and the log will say "exited UNDAMAGED and PARKED (MEC&0xf=1, movement=N) -> clean
      exit" rather than "re-fly/plane-swap". That would mean the movement/flying state is stale
      at removal - note every one of these crashes had a 0x17 StartPlace request arrive ~11ms
      BEFORE the 0x03 delete, and the delete then read flying=False. Same ordering that caused
      the v343 start-place race. Fix that ordering rather than loosening the movement test.

      HOW IT WAS FOUND: only by asking the pilot what he actually did. The wire is genuinely
      ambiguous between a crash and a re-fly - both produce 0x17 then 0x03 then a plane-select -
      and no amount of log reading distinguishes intent. Two sessions were spent inferring.

==============================================================================

v344: [DIAGNOSTIC] name the ACE88 recipients. No behavioural change.

      The msg-88 flood is confirmed from the client side: in messages44, five `in 88'7` land in
      a single millisecond (07:36:43.024) and EVERY one fails - PlayerIndex 3, 1, 7, 4, 0 all
      "AceOrRankChangedCB. PlayerIndex=N not found". Same in messages20 for _AvA_Insane:
      22 failures across indexes 0,1,4,7,8,10,11 out of 83 msg-88 arrivals.

      The broadcast fans out to every session in the room with no check that the recipient was
      ever advertised the subject:
          _targets = [t for t in get_sessions_in_room(s.current_room) if t is not s]
      That is CONSISTENT with the symptom but does not establish the cause - the recipient may
      never have been sent a msg 62 for that player, may have been sent one late, or the index
      may have been recycled. Suppressing sends on that guess risks silencing legitimate rank
      announcements, so this version only makes the question answerable: the ACE88 line now
      lists the recipient pilots by name.

      TO CLOSE IT NEXT SESSION: take one failing PlayerIndex from a client log, find the ACE88
      line that carried it, read off which peers received it, and check whether a PLAYER62
      advertisement for that subject ever went to those peers. That is a direct log-to-log
      cross-reference and it decides between "never advertised" and "advertised but stale".
      Precedent: the same move on FLY23 in v343 turned an undiagnosable subsystem into a
      solved one in a single session. Do not guess at a suppression rule before doing this.

      ALSO NOTED: v343 sat on disk UNDEPLOYED for a full session. server_2_.log shows the last
      start banner as v342 (02:22:44) still serving traffic at 07:37 - the file was v343, the
      process was v342, and an entire test session was diagnosed against the wrong build before
      anyone noticed. Check the banner in the run log against VERSION before analysing anything.

      OPEN, DELIBERATELY NOT FIXED HERE: 5 of 6 deaths in the 07:32 session were classified
      "re-fly/plane-swap, NOT a death" because they carried exit=0x11 (MEC nibble 1); the one
      that counted carried exit=0xa0 (nibble 10). The v232 note says a real crash arrives as
      MissExitCode 26, and each 0x11 exit was preceded ~11ms by a 0x17 StartPlace request -
      which IS the re-fly sequence. Whether those five were genuine crashes or deliberate
      re-flies is unresolved and only the pilot can say. Do NOT flip the classifier on
      inference: it writes lostpilots into the career DB permanently and there is no clean
      rollback if 0x11 really is a plane swap.

