v342: [MODERATOR/SAFETY] the moderator-op probe was claiming ordinary arena-exit deletes.
      Swallowed them for everyone, and EXECUTED them as kill/eject commands for anyone
      holding rights.

      SYMPTOM (reported): shot down, could not respawn ("waiting for a start position on the
      airfield" in an air-spawn arena), exited to HQ to an empty plane list, then the client
      timed out. Client log messages42: 18 x `out 67'1` (the 0x43 room-confirm poll, 1 Hz)
      with no answer, then `Error in Conductor: User has cancelled (-1)` / `Can't exit
      AppSpace`, then `Local layer has timed out (-140)`.

      ROOT CAUSE: the detector took pl[8] as a compound-wrapped moderator opcode without
      checking that the message WAS compound-wrapped. A client's arena-exit bulk delete is
      msg 3 carrying a list of [80 NN] client entries:
          pl = 01 72 00 00 03 00 8001 8002 8003 8005 8006 8007 800b 800d 800e 8004 80
      pl[8] is 0x80, so the delete was claimed as a moderator op.
        * For a NON-moderator: swallowed. The delete never ran. 17 hits across 10 pilots in
          7.5 minutes - ALL41_MAD, Celt, DBD_GCEXTREME, GetsumJ, KILO, MILLHOUSE13, Paddy,
          Snoman, Warrior, _Butterz_. Every one an ordinary arena exit, not an attack.
        * For a pilot WITH rights: EXECUTED. The delete list was parsed as a pilot NAME for
          killp/ejectp/crumblep/nofuelp. Twice in that log (02:04:13 killp?, 02:07:09
          nofuelp?), both landing on "no target" because the bytes decoded to garbage.
          A list that happened to decode to a real pilot name would have killed or ejected
          that player - caused by nothing more than a moderator leaving an arena. This was
          the actual hazard; the hang was the milder half.

      FIX: require the compound marker - a genuinely compound-wrapped message carries 0x00 in
      the sub slot (pl[4]) with the real sub at pl[8]; msg 3 carries 0x03 there. One added
      condition: `pl[4] == 0x00`.
      VERIFIED against real payloads from the log before shipping: the two exit-delete shapes
      are released, while DIRECT (pl[4]=0x80), COMPOUND (pl[4]=0x00) and PREFIXED (bc=0, so
      pl[4]=0x00) moderator commands all still match.
      IF MODERATOR COMMANDS EVER STOP WORKING: a real command is arriving with pl[4] != 0x00.
      Log pl[:12] at that branch and widen the marker - do NOT revert the guard.

      STILL OPEN, NOT FIXED HERE: the client polls 0x43 after returning to the lobby and is
      never sent a JoinToGameAnswer 201, so it hangs and times out. In the same log the only
      201s went to players JOINING (Moira@HQ 02:06:41, ARS_Endo 02:07:39); a player who died
      and went back to lobby got none. _maybe_grant_create_entry covers the arena-CREATION
      flow only. Whether the swallowed delete above was causing this or it is independent is
      NOT established - fix the 201 re-entry grant next and re-test.

      v341 STATUS in this run: TELEM-GUARD fired ZERO times across 7.5 minutes and 14
      players - fully inert, no regression, and no mass CTD recurred.

==============================================================================

v341: [WIRE/CRITICAL] relay_telemetry relayed the WHOLE remaining datagram as one msg-7
      record. One malformed relay killed every client in the arena simultaneously.

      SYMPTOM: 9 players CTD within ~3 seconds, all with the identical engine exception:
          Exception in Fighter Ace engine: bounds error
          class ARR<class NET::OBJECT *,2048>[2144] 0..2047
          file ...\engine\containers\ring.hpp, line 27
      Every client's last network line was `in 7'165`. Normal msg 7 is 84B (T=0x42) or
      86B (T=0x62); 165B is not a legal size for it.

      ROOT CAUSE: the telemetry scan did `c = pl[_off:]` then `_telem = c` - c is the rest
      of the datagram, NOT one record, and nothing truncated it to the declared length.
      A spawn carries the telemetry frame plus trailing bytes, so we forwarded the tail
      wholesale. Receiving clients parse it as a single msg-7 record, read past the real 84B
      into the trailing content, and derive a phantom ONumber from buffer garbage. Anything
      >= 2048 indexes the NET::OBJECT ring out of range and aborts the engine.

      FIX: `_need = 4 + c[0]*16 + (c[1] >> 4)` (Size = bc*16 + (T>>4), full record = 4+Size,
      the same invariant v340 validated on 32/32 real payloads). Relay `c[:_need]`. Frames
      SHORTER than their own header claims are dropped, never relayed. Both conditions log
      once per session under [TELEM-GUARD] - never per-frame, telemetry runs at ~13/s.
      Verified before shipping: 88B datagram -> relays 84B unchanged; 90B -> 86B unchanged;
      169B (the fatal shape) -> 84B instead of 165B; 48B -> dropped.

      EVIDENCE TRAIL (run_20260726_012319, 5 client logs, 1 vcncnet trace):
        * Trigger is a SPAWN: 01:27:23.524 `out 4 spawn -> ServerConfirm Number=267 ident=2`
          (_AvA_Insane, ONumber=0x010b). Object 0x010b has 17 of its 18 frames oversized;
          every other object has 0 across 250+ frames each.
        * The cloud server clock runs ~2s AHEAD of the clients - server 01:27:23.5 is client
          01:27:21.5, which is when the trace shows 84B -> 165B. Always offset-correct before
          correlating cloud-server and client logs.
        * The over-read is directly visible client-side: "Get coord for missing object 1401"
          appears exactly twice in 4 of 5 logs while every other object number is 256..270.
          1401 is survivable, 2144 is not.
        * Ghidra: dispatcher FUN_004f18a0 proves the first appspace byte IS the message type
          and the table is 256 slots at 0xc81ed8, slot = base + id*4 (verified: _DAT_00c82038
          = id 88 = AceOrRank). The bounds assert is a generic ARR<T,2048> macro - see
          FUN_004f1d50 for the exact ring.hpp:27 / "%s[%i] 0..%i" call pair.
        * NOTE FOR NEXT TIME: msg 7's slot (0xc81ef4) is NOT in the FUN_004f1490 registrar -
          the lowest there is id 20 - yet no client ever logged "Unknown Type", so low IDs are
          registered somewhere else. Don't re-derive this; it cost three probes.

      NOT THE CAUSE: v340. Its re-frame path fired 4 times in the whole 23-minute log and
      ZERO times within 30s of the crash; the last two REFRAME lines before the spawn are both
      the v327 normalise path. v340 worked as intended in this same log (38 SUPPLY69,
      3 COLLIDE125, including one re-framed collision and one re-framed supply request).

      REGRESSION METRICS: (1) "Get coord for missing object <N outside 256..270>" in client
      logs must be zero; (2) no msg 7 may ever appear outside the 84-88B band; (3) any
      [TELEM-GUARD] line means a client is sending non-conforming datagrams - worth reading,
      but it is now contained rather than fatal.

==============================================================================

v340: [WIRE] PREFIXED_REFRAME_SUBS - msg 69 (scene-supply) and msg 125 (collision) were
      being dropped 100% of the time in their prefixed form.

      FOUND BY: auditing run_20260725_203749 (4h, 9 pilots, 323 prefixed messages over 22
      distinct subs) and matching every captured message 1:1 against its own handler log line:
          msg 69  (0x45): plain 200/200 handled   PREFIXED  0/29 handled
          msg 125 (0x7d): plain  23/23  handled   PREFIXED  0/3  handled
      Total loss in both cases, not partial. Two apparent prefixed successes on msg 69 were
      timing false positives - each had a PLAIN request from the same pilot within 0.6s that
      accounted for the SUPPLY69 line. Verify that way, not by proximity alone.

      WHY v327 DID NOT COVER THEM: v327 normalises sub to 0x00 so the pl[8] double-wrap
      fallbacks fire. That only helps subs which HAVE such a fallback (chat 0x14, team-select
      0x44, leave 0x40, delete 0x03, 0x17, 0x1f). 0x45 and 0x7d have none, so normalising sub
      to 0 just guarantees no handler ever matches and the message dies silently. That is the
      real definition of the '19 uncovered subs' from the v33x audit: not 'no prefixed guard'
      but 'no pl[8] reader'.

      FIX: PREFIXED_REFRAME_SUBS = {0x03, 0x45, 0x7d} - these get the FULL re-frame (pl = pl[4:])
      that 0x03 has had since v326, instead of the sub-normalisation. The existing plain-path
      handlers then receive a plain frame and needed NO changes at all.
      This is NOT the blanket re-frame the v327 comment warns against. v327's hazard is shifting
      bytes under a handler that already reads pl[8]; these two have no pl[8] reader, which is
      precisely why they were dead, so nothing currently being read can shift. The set is
      explicit rather than a default - empty it to revert to pure v327 behaviour.

      VERIFIED STATICALLY on all 32 captured prefixed payloads before shipping:
        * the _pfx guard detects 32/32
        * after pl[4:] all 32 satisfy len == 4 + bc*16 + (T>>4)
        * every resulting (T,len) shape is one the plain handler has already processed
          (msg 69: 29/29 shapes, T=0x32/0x52/0x72/0x92/0xb2; msg 125: 3/3, T=0x72)
      NOTE the frame-length invariant is len == 4 + bc*16 + (T>>4), NOT bc*16 + (T>>4). Size
      excludes the [bc][T][00][00] header. Getting that wrong makes every PLAIN message look
      like a mismatch too, which is the tell.

      ALSO CORRECTED: the 'byte-swapped cmd' anomaly (0x0312 vs 0x1203) is not a bug - the
      v327 layout note already documents pl[2:4] as cmd, BE u16. Nothing to chase there.

      NOT DONE, deliberately: 0xd4 (LEAVE) and 0xd7 (SetArena) were the v33x audit's prime
      suspects for the escape-to-leave bug, but they appear ZERO times in the entire run,
      prefixed or plain. All 16 in-game leaves went through msg 64 (0x40), which already has a
      pl[8] fallback and fired 5/5 on its prefixed arrivals. Those two audit entries are
      unfalsifiable from this capture and the paths look dead - do not fix them blind.
      msg 28 (0x1c) is a weak signal worth a look: 21 prefixed seen, ~76% handled vs 94% plain.

