v334: [PLANES] The Me-163B stayed visible to non-staff - the catalog filter was never running.
      Same omission as msg 28 in v333, in a second place. The 0x3a catalog filter - which is
      what ACTUALLY hides planes, since the hangar gates on plane-ID membership in this echoed
      list - was gated on `sub == 0x3a` alone. When the client sends its catalog in the PREFIXED
      wrapper, v327 normalises sub to 0x00, so the filter never fired and the FULL unfiltered
      catalog was echoed straight back. The 0x3a catalog is definitely seen prefixed in practice
      ("cmd=0x00e2 ... inner type=0xa2 sub=0x3a" appears in the REFRAME notes).
      TWO offset bugs came with it, exactly as in v333: in the prefixed form pl[0]/pl[1] are the
      counter and cmd bytes, NOT bc/T, and the ID list starts 4 bytes later - so reading the
      header from the wrong bytes gives a nonsense size and slices garbage. Base is now 4 for the
      prefixed form, 0 otherwise.
      ALSO CLOSED: the block was gated on a valid s.nation, so a pilot browsing the hangar
      BEFORE picking a country got the full catalog, Komet included. The side filter needs a
      nation; the staff restriction does not. It now runs whenever there is either a side to
      filter by or something staff-only to remove.
      Verified on both wire forms: ids [47,48,49,118] -> kept [47,48,49] either way, and under
      the old code the prefixed form did not fire at all.

      PATTERN WORTH REMEMBERING: v327 made prefixed messages routable by normalising sub to 0x00
      so the pl[8] fallbacks fire - but only the handlers that ALREADY had a pl[8] fallback
      benefited. Any handler still testing `sub == X` alone silently stops working the moment
      the client uses the prefixed wrapper for that message. msg 28 (v333) and 0x3a (v334) were
      both this. Still testing `sub == X` with no pl[8] path, and worth auditing next: the two
      other 0x3a handlers (SEND_EXIT_UNRELIABLE reattach, SEND_CAREER_ON_HQ), and anything else
      that greps as `sub == 0x..` without a matching `pl[8] ==`.

