v333: [REGRESSION + MP] Reverted the v332 GAME_DEF plane mask (it CTD'd the client), and
      fixed prefixed damage being silently dropped.

      (1) *** v332 REGRESSION - ARENA PAGE HARD CTD FOR NON-STAFF PILOTS. ***
      v332 hid staff-only aircraft two ways; one of them was wrong. Setting GAME_DEF plane
      record byte 0 to 0x00 crashes the client on the arena page. From the crash dump
      (messages30):
          Exception in Fighter Ace engine: logical error
          Assertion failed (Len==0)
          file ...\GameDef.cpp, line 1902
      That is the GAME_DEF DESERIALISER - a consumption mismatch, the same family as the
      GameDef.cpp:2076 Source.End() assert already noted in the LZ section. NOT the side picker.
      So the v332 reasoning was wrong: byte 0 is not merely a camp bitmask the renderer
      consults, it participates in how the record is PARSED, and 0x00 makes the parser consume
      a different number of bytes and trip a length assert. A purely semantic "no camp" value
      would have greyed the plane out or crashed in the picker - it would not fail a LENGTH
      assertion in the parser.
      The warning sign was there and was missed: apply_plane_teams only ever writes 0x1f or a
      single set bit, NEVER zero, and stock data never contains 0x00 there either. v332
      introduced a value the format does not allow.
      CONCLUSION: byte 0 can select WHICH nations may fly a plane; it cannot express "nobody".
      STAFF_HIDE_VIA_GAMEDEF=False, so the 212 is now byte-identical to v331 for staff and
      non-staff alike. The Me-163B restriction still works, on the 0x3a catalog trim alone -
      which is the game's own designed filter path here (PLANE_FILTER_VIA_CATALOG is True, so
      apply_plane_teams deliberately leaves byte 0 at 0x1f and lets the catalog echo filter).
      TESTING LESSON, recorded because it caused this: v332 was verified live by a pilot WITH
      an @FA tag - which hides nothing and therefore exercises none of the new code. The
      routing logic was tested; that the modified GAME_DEF still PARSES was not. Any future
      change here must be tested on a NON-staff pilot.

      (2) PREFIXED DAMAGE (msg 28 / sub 0x1c) WAS SILENTLY DROPPED - shots that registered on
      the shooter's screen never reached the victim.
      Damage was gated on `sub == 0x1c` alone. For a prefixed message sub is pl[4] (the inner
      bc), which v327 normalises to 0x00 so the pl[8] fallbacks can fire - but damage never had
      one. Every other double-wrapped type (chat 0x14, team 0x44, leave 0x40, delete 0x03,
      0x17, 0x1f) already had a pl[8] fallback; 0x1c was simply missed when v327 went in.
      EVIDENCE (run_20260725_203749 + messages69): ALL41_MAD's hits arrived as
          cmd=0x0542 PFX type=0x82 sub=0x1c len=32
          pl=00170542|01820000|1c0401090102...      <- pl[8]=0x1c, the real sub
      and landed in SUPPLY-CAP, unrecognised. The server logged not ONE damage report from
      ALL41_MAD all session (senders were only MILLHOUSE13, Snoman and Warrior), while
      Warrior's client logged zero "You have been hit" lines.
      TWO parts to the fix, both needed:
        * the guard now also accepts pl[8] == 0x1c
        * the record offsets were HARDCODED to 5 and 7, which are only right for the
          non-prefixed form. In the prefixed form the record sits 4 bytes later. Base 5 on the
          real packet yields victim=0x0082 hunter=0x1c00 - garbage matching no peer - so even
          with the guard fixed the damage would have been attributed to nobody. _dbase is now
          9 for the prefixed form, 5 otherwise. Verified on both real captures: each decodes to
          victim=0x0104 hunter=0x0109, and 0x0109 is ALL41_MAD.

